Jay Z officially entered the streaming music wars Monday with the launch of Tidal, the subscription service he recently bought for $56 million. The goal, he says, is to put the majority of the company in artists' hands, symbolic in a business where musicians traditionally have little say on how their work is consumed. To underscore the point, Tidal's launch featured pop music's A-list, including Madonna, Jack White, Rihanna, and Kanye West.
But for all the fanfare, is the service worth trying? Well, that depends how you feel about Spotify. Both services have similarly sized catalogs (30 million songs on Spotify; 25 million on Tidal), but with 15 million paying subscribers, versus 17,000 on Tidal, the latter faces stiff competition. Sure, each Tidal artist received a 3 percent stake in the company in exchange for exclusive content. But will it matter if consumers don't switch? Not likely. Here's a side-by-side comparison of both platforms to help you decide.
Pricing
Unlike Spotify, whose monthly subscription costs $9.99, Tidal offers no free tier after a 30-day trial. Consumers have the choice between paying $19.99 per month for so-called CD-quality streams or $9.99 per month for a standard tier. At least with Spotify you can stream randomized playlists by phone or select tracks on desktop for free.
Tidal: 0
Spotify: 1
Moral imperative
"Water is free. Music is $6 but no one wants to pay for music. You should drink free water from the tap—it's a beautiful thing," Jay Z told the New York Times. "And if you want to hear the most beautiful song, then support the artist." That's nice in theory, but the artists in Jay's circle hardly need his help. As Spotify revealed last month, Beyoncé, Rihanna, and Nicki Minaj—all of whom were at yesterday's launch—are among the most-streamed female artists on the site. Rihanna is Spotify's most-streamed woman for the third year in a row, thanks to hits like "The Monster," which has been streamed over 200 million times. Still, considering Spotify pays as little as 0.6 cents per stream, it's not like there's much of a choice here.
