Q. Can you provide us with a history of the Anaheim Plaza? Wasn't it an indoor mall, then converted to an outdoor center?
– Mario Luna, Anaheim
A. Mr. Anaheim can personally vouch for the fact that it was, indeed, an indoor mall in the late 1970s and '80s. I never worked there, as many of my friends did, but I do remember spending many hours shopping for back-to-school clothes with my mom at the anchor stores, including The Broadway, Mervyn's and Robinson's department stores. (Mr. Anaheim probably spent a few too many quarters – and hours – playing Frogger and Centipede in the arcade there, too.)
But I asked a better source, with more history in Anaheim, Stephen Faessel, 62, a local historian and Anaheim planning commissioner. He remembers going there when it opened in 1955 – the same year that Disneyland opened its gates.
Back then, it was just The Broadway and a few accompanying stores that shoppers had to go outside to get to. And it was a bid deal.
"It had been nothing more than an orange grove before that, so for those of us old-timers in Anaheim, our mouths were agape at this shiny new, modern shopping center," Faessel said.
About a decade later, Robinsons was added and the mall was enclosed in the 1970s. It flourished for a couple of decades until Robinsons moved out around 1987 – focusing on the growing Mainplace Mall in Santa Ana instead. Anaheim Plaza stores suffered and began closing down for good.
In 1993, original anchor The Broadway closed and the entire mall – other than Mervyn's – was demolished for redevelopment.
The demolition came despite some community opposition from people who pleaded to save the mall. It was converted to a center for big-box stores, including Walmart. Mervyn's later became a Forever 21.
"It's still a nice little neighborhood shopping center with Marie Calender's, the AAA office and all," Faessel said. "The shame of it all, though, is that a town like Anaheim wasn't able to sustain an elite shopping mall."
